+      <article class="journal-copy" id="journal-text" aria-label="Our bean sourcing process">
+        <p>A memorable cup begins long before the roaster warms up. Our sourcing process starts with a simple question:
+          who grew this coffee, and what makes their harvest special? We look for producers and cooperatives who can
+          tell us the story behind a lot, from the region and elevation to the varieties planted and the way the
+          cherries were processed. Knowing those details helps us choose coffees for their character and understand
+          the work that went into them.</p>
+
+        <p>Relationships matter as much as tasting notes. We work with sourcing partners who connect us to growers,
+          share information about how coffee is purchased, and help us plan beyond a single harvest. Our aim is to
+          build lasting relationships rather than chase the lowest price. We consider the cost of careful farming,
+          harvesting, and processing alongside cup quality, and ask questions about how the people doing that work
+          are paid. A beautiful coffee should have a story we can stand behind.</p>
+
+        <p>We also look closely at how a farm cares for its surroundings. Shade trees, healthy soil, thoughtful water
+          use, and responsible processing all belong in the conversation. Conditions differ from one growing region
+          to another, so we ask about the practices that make sense locally instead of assuming that one approach
+          fits every farm. We value honest information about both progress and challenges, and we keep learning
+          alongside our partners.</p>
+
+        <p>Before committing to a coffee, we roast small samples and taste them side by side in a process called
+          cupping. We look for sweetness, balance, a clean finish, and a distinctive sense of place. We taste more
+          than once, compare our notes, and think about how the coffee will feel in your everyday brew. Seasonality
+          guides those decisions: as fresh harvests arrive, our selection changes to celebrate beans at their best
+          rather than promise the same flavor all year.</p>
+
+        <p>Once a lot reaches our roastery, we check its condition and develop a roast profile that brings out what
+          we loved at the tasting table. Each batch is recorded, tasted, and adjusted when needed. From there, we
+          keep the journey to your cup as short as we can, roasting in small batches and sharing brewing guidance.
+          Sourcing is never a finished checklist for us. It is an ongoing practice of asking better questions,
+          choosing carefully, and honoring the people behind every bean.</p>
+      </article>
